Subject: [naga wgsl-out] spell f32::MAX exactly

Verified against trunk: `Literal::F32` is still `write!(self.out, "{value}f")?`
with no special case, and the `i32::MIN` arm quoted below is present there too.
Patch context taken from the crates.io 30.0.0 source; not yet compiled or run
against naga's snapshot suite.

`write!(self.out, "{value}f")` uses Rust's `Display` for `f32`, which emits the
shortest decimal that round-trips *as an f32*. For `f32::MAX` that decimal is
`340282350000000000000000000000000000000` — it rounds back to `f32::MAX`, but as
a real number it is greater than it.

WGSL parses a literal as `AbstractFloat` before converting, so the emitted value
is out of range for its own type. Tint rejects it; naga's front end accepts it,
which is why a round trip looks clean in-tree.

This is the same class of bug the `i32::MIN` arm below already handles, and it
has the same shape of fix: one value cannot be spelled the obvious way.

`f32::MAX` is the only f32 with this property — a scan of the two million
values below it found no other whose `Display` output exceeds `f32::MAX` —
so this affects exactly two literals and no existing snapshot that does not
contain one.

`f16` needs nothing: `f16::MAX` is 65504, exactly representable as a decimal.

This does NOT fix #9907, and should not be expected to. That issue is about the
39-digit positional expansion failing WebKit's lexer, which is a formatting
question; this is about the value being out of range. After this patch `3e38`
still prints as `300000000000000000000000000000000000000f`. The two changes are
compatible and both are wanted.

Effect on output, measured:

    f32::MAX   340282350000000000000000000000000000000f  ->  3.4028234663852886e38f
    -f32::MAX  -34028235000000000000000000000000000000 0f ->  -3.4028234663852886e38f
    0.1        0.1f                                      ->  0.1f          (unchanged)
    3e38       300000000000000000000000000000000000000f  ->  unchanged     (see #9907)

The new spelling for `f32::MAX` is accepted by both Chrome 151 and Firefox 153,
measured through `createShaderModule`.

--- a/naga/src/back/wgsl/writer.rs
+++ b/naga/src/back/wgsl/writer.rs
@@
-                crate::Literal::F32(value) => write!(self.out, "{value}f")?,
+                crate::Literal::F32(value) => {
+                    // `340282350000000000000000000000000000000f` is not valid
+                    // WGSL. `Display` for `f32` emits the shortest decimal that
+                    // round-trips as an `f32`, and for `f32::MAX` that decimal
+                    // denotes a larger value than `f32::MAX` itself. WGSL reads
+                    // a literal as `AbstractFloat` first, where it is out of
+                    // range, so the extremes must be spelled exactly.
+                    if value == f32::MAX || value == f32::MIN {
+                        write!(self.out, "{:e}f", value as f64)?;
+                    } else {
+                        write!(self.out, "{value}f")?;
+                    }
+                }
